WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.338
The Steadfast Tin Soldier

00:00:03.038 --> 00:00:05.793
There were once five-and-twenty tin soldiers.

00:00:06.143 --> 00:00:09.600
They were all brothers, born of the same old tin spoon.

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:16.105
They shouldered their muskets and looked straight ahead of them, splendid in their uniforms, all red and blue.

00:00:16.455 --> 00:00:26.791
The very first thing in the world that they heard was, 'Tin soldiers!' A small boy shouted it and clapped his hands as the lid was lifted off their box on his birthday.

00:00:27.141 --> 00:00:29.407
He immediately set them up on the table.

00:00:30.057 --> 00:00:33.462
All the soldiers looked exactly alike except one.

00:00:33.812 --> 00:00:37.163
He looked a little different as he had been cast last of all.

00:00:37.513 --> 00:00:40.206
The tin was short, so he had only one leg.

00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:45.654
But there he stood, as steady on one leg as any of the other soldiers on their two.

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:48.897
But just you see, he'll be the remarkable one.

00:00:49.547 --> 00:00:57.387
On the table with the soldiers were many other playthings, and one that no eye could miss was a marvelous castle of cardboard.

00:00:57.737 --> 00:01:01.269
It had little windows through which you could look right inside it.

00:01:01.619 --> 00:01:07.188
And in front of the castle were miniature trees around a little mirror supposed to represent a lake.

00:01:07.538 --> 00:01:12.595
But prettiest of all was the little lady who stood in the open doorway of the castle.

00:01:12.945 --> 00:01:16.997
Though she was a paper doll, she wore a dress of the fluffiest gauze.

00:01:17.347 --> 00:01:24.664
A tiny blue ribbon went over her shoulder for a scarf, and in the middle of it shone a spangle that was as big as her face.

00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:38.444
The little lady held out both her arms, as a ballet dancer does, and one leg was lifted so high behind her that the tin soldier couldn't see it at all, and he supposed she must have only one leg, as he did.

00:01:38.794 --> 00:01:40.951
'That would be a wife for me,' he thought.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:47.680
When the evening came the other tin soldiers were put away in their box, and the people of the house went to bed.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.460
Now the toys began to play among themselves at visits, and battles, and at giving balls.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:02:00.478
The tin soldiers rattled about in their box, for they wanted to play too, but they could not get the lid open.

00:02:00.828 --> 00:02:06.001
The nutcracker turned somersaults, and the slate pencil squeaked out jokes on the slate.

00:02:06.351 --> 00:02:12.153
The toys made such a noise that they woke up the canary bird, who made them a speech, all in verse.

00:02:12.503 --> 00:02:16.760
The only two who stayed still were the tin soldier and the little dancer.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.408
Not once did he take his eyes off her.

00:02:20.058 --> 00:02:22.416
Then the clock struck twelve and — clack!

00:02:22.766 --> 00:02:24.974
— up popped the lid of the snuffbox.

00:02:25.324 --> 00:02:30.358
But there was no snuff in it — out bounced a little black bogey, a jack-in-the-box.

00:02:30.708 --> 00:02:32.458
'Tin soldier,' he said.

00:02:32.808 --> 00:02:37.747
'Will you please keep your eyes to yourself?' The tin soldier pretended not to hear.

00:02:38.097 --> 00:02:40.820
The bogey said, 'Just you wait till tomorrow.'

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.654
But when morning came, and the children got up, the soldier was set on the window ledge.

00:02:47.004 --> 00:02:56.338
And whether the bogey did it, or there was a gust of wind, all of a sudden the window flew open and the soldier pitched out headlong from the third floor.

00:02:56.988 --> 00:03:05.766
He fell at breathtaking speed and landed cap first, with his bayonet buried between the paving stones and his one leg stuck straight in the air.

00:03:06.116 --> 00:03:14.851
The housemaid and the little boy ran down to look for him and, though they nearly stepped on the tin soldier, they walked right past without seeing him.

00:03:15.201 --> 00:03:24.264
If the soldier had called, 'Here I am!' they would surely have found him, but he thought it contemptible to raise an uproar while he was wearing his uniform.

00:03:24.914 --> 00:03:26.560
Soon it began to rain.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:31.476
The drops fell faster and faster, until they came down by the bucketful.

00:03:31.826 --> 00:03:35.656
As soon as the rain let up, along came two young rapscallions.

00:03:36.006 --> 00:03:39.087
'Hi, look!' one of them said, 'there's a tin soldier.

00:03:39.437 --> 00:03:40.945
Let's send him sailing.'

00:03:41.595 --> 00:03:52.153
They made a boat out of newspaper, put the tin soldier in the middle of it, and away he went down the gutter with the two young rapscallions running beside him and clapping their hands.

00:03:52.503 --> 00:03:53.719
High heavens!

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:57.944
How the waves splashed, and how fast the water ran down the gutter.

00:03:58.294 --> 00:04:05.226
The paper boat pitched, and tossed, and sometimes it whirled about so rapidly that it made the soldier's head spin.

00:04:05.576 --> 00:04:07.458
But he stood as steady as ever.

00:04:07.808 --> 00:04:12.566
Never once flinching, he kept his eyes front, and carried his gun shoulder-high.

00:04:13.216 --> 00:04:18.017
Suddenly the boat rushed under a long plank where the gutter was boarded over.

00:04:18.367 --> 00:04:21.162
It was as dark as the soldier's own box.

00:04:21.512 --> 00:04:24.255
'Where can I be going?' the soldier wondered.

00:04:24.605 --> 00:04:27.083
'This must be that black bogey's revenge.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:28.286
Ah!

00:04:28.636 --> 00:04:34.206
if only I had the little lady with me, it could be twice as dark here for all that I would care.'

00:04:34.856 --> 00:04:38.429
Out popped a great water rat who lived under the gutter plank.

00:04:38.779 --> 00:04:41.083
'Have you a passport?' said the rat.

00:04:41.433 --> 00:04:45.840
'Hand it over.' The soldier kept quiet and held his musket tighter.

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:53.347
On rushed the boat, and the rat came right after it, gnashing his teeth as he called to the sticks and straws: 'Halt him!

00:04:53.697 --> 00:04:54.743
Stop him!

00:04:55.093 --> 00:04:56.653
He didn't pay his toll.

00:04:57.003 --> 00:04:58.874
He hasn't shown his passport.'

00:04:59.524 --> 00:05:01.501
The boat plunged into the whirlpool.

00:05:01.851 --> 00:05:08.262
The poor tin soldier stood as staunch as he could, and no one can say that he so much as blinked an eye.

00:05:08.612 --> 00:05:11.121
Thrice and again the boat spun around.

00:05:11.471 --> 00:05:14.250
It filled to the top — and was bound to sink.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:22.019
The water was up to his neck and still the boat went down, deeper, deeper, deeper, and the paper got soft and limp.

00:05:22.369 --> 00:05:24.463
Then the water rushed over his head.

00:05:24.813 --> 00:05:36.044
He thought of the pretty little dancer whom he'd never see again, and in his ears rang an old, old song: 'Farewell, farewell, O warrior brave, nobody can from Death thee save.'

00:05:36.694 --> 00:05:41.217
And now the paper boat broke beneath him, and the soldier sank right through.

00:05:41.567 --> 00:05:45.525
And just at that moment he was swallowed by a most enormous fish.

00:05:46.175 --> 00:05:47.028
My!

00:05:47.378 --> 00:05:49.600
how dark it was inside that fish.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:55.870
It was darker than under the gutter-plank and it was so cramped, but the tin soldier still was staunch.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:00.281
He lay there full length, soldier fashion, with musket to shoulder.

00:06:00.931 --> 00:06:04.729
Then the fish flopped and floundered in a most unaccountable way.

00:06:05.079 --> 00:06:11.160
Finally it was perfectly still, and after a while something struck through him like a flash of lightning.

00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:16.269
The tin soldier saw daylight again, and he heard a voice say, 'The Tin Soldier!'

00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:23.986
The fish had been caught, carried to market, bought, and brought to a kitchen where the cook cut him open with her big knife.

00:06:24.336 --> 00:06:29.307
She picked the soldier up bodily between her two fingers, and carried him off upstairs.

00:06:29.657 --> 00:06:37.486
Everyone wanted to see this remarkable traveler who had traveled about in a fish's stomach, but the tin soldier took no pride in it.

00:06:38.136 --> 00:06:46.899
They put him on the table and — lo and behold, what curious things can happen in this world — there he was, back in the same room as before.

00:06:47.249 --> 00:06:54.684
He saw the same children, the same toys were on the table, and there was the same fine castle with the pretty little dancer.

00:06:55.034 --> 00:06:58.364
She still balanced on one leg, with the other raised high.

00:06:58.714 --> 00:07:00.370
She too was steadfast.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.725
That touched the soldier so deeply that he would have cried tin tears, only soldiers never cry.

00:07:07.075 --> 00:07:10.640
He looked at her, and she looked at him, and never a word was said.

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:18.725
Just as things were going so nicely for them, one of the little boys snatched up the tin soldier and threw him into the stove.

00:07:19.075 --> 00:07:21.042
He did it for no reason at all.

00:07:21.392 --> 00:07:24.755
That black bogey in the snuffbox must have put him up to it.

00:07:25.105 --> 00:07:27.806
The tin soldier stood there dressed in flames.

00:07:28.456 --> 00:07:33.854
He felt a terrible heat, but whether it came from the flames or from his love he didn't know.

00:07:34.204 --> 00:07:38.706
He looked at the little lady, and she looked at him, and he felt himself melting.

00:07:39.056 --> 00:07:43.377
But still he stood steadfast, with his musket held trim on his shoulder.

00:07:43.727 --> 00:07:45.459
Then the door blew open.

00:07:45.809 --> 00:07:47.957
A puff of wind struck the dancer.

00:07:48.307 --> 00:07:54.238
She flew like a sylph, straight into the fire with the soldier, blazed up in a flash, and was gone.

00:07:54.888 --> 00:07:57.491
The tin soldier melted, all in a lump.

00:07:57.841 --> 00:08:03.325
The next day, when a servant took up the ashes she found him in the shape of a little tin heart.

00:08:03.675 --> 00:08:09.649
But of the pretty dancer nothing was left except her spangle, and it was burned as black as a coal.

00:08:09.999 --> 00:08:12.071
And that is the end of the tale.

